  {
    "kind": "youtube#playlistItem",
    "etag": "\"nxOHAKTVB7baOKsQgTtJIyGxcs8/MdbTUqCxLttbQr5qn4VPLd1niXE\"",
    "id": "VVVzS3hacXJaYlY1UjlFaEhLOC1hQnp3LmYxVTZNQ0R1Z1dF",
    "snippet": {
      "publishedAt": "2015-11-04T00:13:04.000Z",
      "channelId": "UCsKxZqrZbV5R9EhHK8-aBzw",
      "title": "Stoyan Stefanov – Rewriting Facebook comments plugin with React and Flux – Falsy Values 2015",
      "description": "http://falsyvalues.com/\n\nFacebook’s comments plugin is a third-party commenting system with user front-end and a moderation tool. This session describes what we did to rewrite and relaunch the offering using modern tools – React, Flux, Flow, ES6 and beyond. Learn about the architecture decisions as well as day-to-day JavaScript coding that allows for less code (which almost certainly means fewer bugs) and happy developers.\n\nRecently Facebook launched a new iteration of the third-party commenting plugin and a moderation tool to go with it. Both were rebuilt from the ground up using Facebook’s latest open-source front-end technologies. This talk is about the details of building of the front-end of the new apps including the big-picture architecture as well as the nitty-gritty details of everyday coding in the world of typed JavaScript.",
      "thumbnails": {
        "default": {
